# PhotoPipeline — M2-T2 ThreadSanitizer suppressions (thread sanitizer only).
#
# Exact invocation used for every M2-T2 TSan run (also encoded in the `tsan` test preset):
#   TSAN_OPTIONS="halt_on_error=0:second_deadlock_stack=1:ignore_interceptors_accesses=1
#                 :suppressions=<repo>/tools/tsan.supp:print_suppressions=1"
#   ctest --test-dir build/m2-t2 --output-on-failure        # 24/24, 0 reports
#
# SYNTAX FACTS (verified with the positive control .cache/tmp/m2-t2/race_control.cpp):
#   * a line is `type:pattern`; everything after the colon is the pattern, so INLINE COMMENTS ARE
#     NOT SUPPORTED (they become part of the pattern and then never match) — every argument below
#     sits on its own comment line above its entry;
#   * the pattern is matched as a SUBSTRING of the DEMANGLED function name (mangled-name patterns
#     never match); `race_top:` matches only the top frame, `race:` any frame of either stack.
#
# ===========================================================================================
# ROOT CAUSE OF EVERY SUPPRESSED REPORT: UNINSTRUMENTED THIRD-PARTY CODE
# ===========================================================================================
# The vcpkg debug static libraries (OpenImageIO, zlib, libtiff, …) and the prebuilt Qt/GLib DSOs
# are compiled WITHOUT `-fsanitize=thread`. TSan's happens-before tracking cannot see the
# acquire/release pairs they perform with their own primitives: an instrumented build lowers
# those to `__tsan_acquire`/`__tsan_release` calls, while the binaries contain the bare
# instructions. The locks still order the accesses correctly at run time; TSan records no edge
# and reports the pair. Local evidence (objdump on build/m2-t2 binaries, demangled for reading):
#   * OIIO spin lock: `OpenImageIO::v3_1::spin_mutex::try_lock` is a raw `xchg %dl,(%rax)` with
#     no TSan callback; `spin_mutex::lock()` spins on it.
#   * ustring table: `TableRepMap<256,4096>::lookup` enters through
#     `spin_rw_mutex::read_lock_guard::read_lock_guard`, `TableRepMap<256,4096>::insert` through
#     `spin_rw_mutex::write_lock_guard::write_lock_guard`.
#   * thread-pool thread-id map: `thread_pool::Impl::register_worker` holds
#     `spin_mutex::lock_guard` around `tsl::robin_map<std::thread::id,int>::operator[]`.
#   * thread-pool job queue: `pvt::ThreadsafeQueue<std::function<void(int)>*>::push` holds
#     `std::unique_lock<spin_mutex>` across the deque push; the worker's `pop` takes the same
#     spin_mutex — that release/acquire edge covers the queued `std::function` and the
#     `std::packaged_task` control block it owns.
#
# Independent reproduction (PhotoPipeline threading is not involved):
#   .cache/m2-t2-tsan/oiio_single.log — ONE application thread, concurrency only from OIIO's own
#   default thread pool, still reports the same families; .cache/m2-t2-tsan/oiio_only.log — two
#   app threads and ZERO PhotoPipeline TUs, same result.
#
# WHY `ignore_interceptors_accesses=1` IS PART OF THE INVOCATION (and what it does NOT hide):
#   The Qt/GLib/allocator reports observed without it are pairs of *interceptor-internal*
#   accesses: TSan's malloc/realloc/free/write interceptors model allocator and file-descriptor
#   bookkeeping as memory writes, and Qt's own pool/runnable handoff (QAtomicPointer, QArrayData
#   refcounts inside the uninstrumented DSO) provides no visible edge for them. The flag drops
#   those interceptor-side accesses; it does not touch access instrumentation of application or
#   library code. Proof that real races are still caught with the flag enabled (both controls in
#   .cache/m2-t2-tsan/): race_control.log — direct racy ++ on a non-atomic pp global, 1 report;
#   race_control2.log — memcpy-mediated race (writer memcpy vs. reader), 1 report.
#
# POSITIVE CONTROL FOR THIS FILE (it is not a blanket race-detection disable):
#   .cache/m2-t2-tsan/race_control.log and race_control2.log still report with this file loaded,
#   because no suppressed library frame appears in their stacks.
#
# HIT COUNTS in the M2-T2 verification set (print_suppressions=1): see the M2-T2 report §TSan.
#   Active entries: `std::packaged_task` (343 enc_oiio / 36 scheduler / 18 thumbs / 10 pipeline)
#   and `OpenImageIO::v3_1::thread_pool` (172 / 18 / 10 / 5); every other entry is marked
#   DORMANT — 0 hits in the final configuration, kept because the identical pairs were observed
#   in the pre-flag baseline logs (.cache/m2-t2-tsan/base-*.log, oiio_only.log) and may reappear
#   with different timing on other machines (CI).

# --- ACTIVE: OIIO default thread pool (queue, registry, pool state) -------------------------
# HB argument: the pool's job queue, thread-id registry and running state are all mutated under
# the pool's spin_mutex (ThreadsafeQueue::push/pop use std::unique_lock<spin_mutex>;
# Impl::register_worker/run_one_task/resize/stop/init take spin_mutex::lock_guard). The
# producer's unlock publishes the queued std::function, the pooled bookkeeping and the task
# control block; the worker's lock acquires them.
race:OpenImageIO::v3_1::thread_pool
# HB argument: refcount/control block of the std::packaged_task that thread_pool::push creates
# for every job and the worker destroys after running it — same queue spin_mutex edge. TSan's
# top frame is usually operator new/delete or ~__shared_count, so match any frame.
# PhotoPipeline TUs never name std::packaged_task / std::future (verified by grep), so this
# cannot hide an application-data race.
race:std::packaged_task

# --- ACTIVE: Qt queued invocation (QMetaObject::invokeMethod + functor) --------------------
# What races: the worker thread copies the callable and its captures into the heap object that
# Qt's event posting publishes (frame `QCallableObject`, qobjectdefs_impl.h) while the receiving
# thread invokes that same object through the QMetaCallEvent (frames `QtPrivate::FunctorCall` /
# `impl`). The captures are therefore OUR values, but the *publication* is Qt's.
# HB argument: QCoreApplication::postEvent publishes the event through the receiving thread's
# post-event list. On the uncontended path that list is guarded by a QMutex (pthread-based, so
# TSan sees lock/unlock and the report cannot happen); on the contended path - receiver busy
# processing events, i.e. exactly the oversubscribed case - Qt 6 appends to a lock-free pending
# list and the receiver fetches it with QAtomicPointer release/acquire. libQt6Core is a prebuilt
# DSO without TSan instrumentation, so those atomics produce no shadow edge and the pair is
# reported even though the capture writes are properly published before the receiver's reads.
# Evidence: `.cache/m2-t2-tsan/qtqueued-*.log` - control .cache/tmp/m2-t2/qt_queued.cpp contains
# ZERO PhotoPipeline code (two std::threads posting lambdas to the main thread with
# QMetaObject::invokeMethod(..., Qt::QueuedConnection)) and reproduces this family 5/5 runs under
# `taskset -c 0,1` (9 reports each); the same family shows up in the Thumbnailer harness only
# under the same 2-CPU oversubscription (taskset-thumbs-3.log) and never on an idle 16-core host
# (47 clean runs). Pattern scope: ONLY the functor-copy frame of a queued invocation matches, so
# an application-data race that does not go through Qt's callable machinery is still reported.
race:QCallableObject

# --- DORMANT VARIANTS OF THE SAME TWO FAMILIES (0 hits in the final run set) ----------------
# HB argument: allocator frame of the packaged_task control block (the allocator's template
# argument names the type), reached only under the pool spin_mutex.
race:std::__new_allocator<std::_Sp_counted_ptr_inplace<std::packaged_task
# HB argument: in-place control block of that same packaged_task, freed by the worker after the
# queue pop.
race:std::_Sp_counted_ptr_inplace<std::packaged_task
# HB argument: std::future shared state (`_Task_state`) of a pool task: allocated by the producer,
# read/updated by the worker. libstdc++ guards it with its own recursive mutex, but the publish
# edge from push() into the queue is the pool spin_mutex above. PhotoPipeline uses no std::future.
race:std::__future_base::_Task_state
# HB argument: OIIO parallel_image task callables — the 5-arg (id + ROI range) form, its 4-arg
# bound form and the ROI-carrying form; all are std::function objects queued through the pool
# spin_mutex. Those exact signatures are OIIO's; PhotoPipeline declares none of them.
race:std::function<void (int, long, long, long, long)>
race:std::function<void (long, long, long, long)>
race:std::function<void (OpenImageIO::v3_1::ROI)>
# HB argument: parallel_image dispatcher owning those callables.
race:OpenImageIO::v3_1::ImageBufAlgo::parallel_image

# --- DORMANT: OIIO global ustring interning table ------------------------------------------
# HB argument: writers mutate the bucket under spin_rw_mutex::write_lock_guard and release the
# rw-lock word when the guard dies; readers take spin_rw_mutex::read_lock_guard before touching
# the bucket, so release-on-unlock / acquire-on-lock orders bucket bytes and TableRep payload.
race:TableRepMap
# HB argument: same rw-lock, non-template path (ustring::make_unique inserting; TableRep ctor
# filling the string payload); the racing access may be the strncmp/memcpy interceptor above it.
race:OpenImageIO::v3_1::ustring

# --- DORMANT: OIIO thread-id robin_map nodes ------------------------------------------------
# HB argument: every access to `tsl::robin_map<std::thread::id,int>` in the pool happens while
# holding the pool spin_mutex (see Impl::register_worker disassembly); unlock publishes the node
# writes, the next lock acquires them.
race:tsl::detail_robin_hash
# HB argument: node de/allocation frame of that same robin_map, reached only under the spin_mutex.
race:std::__new_allocator<tsl::detail_robin_hash
# HB argument: robin_map bucket relocation/move under the pool spin_mutex.
race:std::swap<std::thread::id>
# HB argument: robin_map key comparison under the pool spin_mutex.
race:std::equal_to<int>::operator()

# --- DORMANT: OIIO ImageBuf / ImageSpec handoff into pool tasks ------------------------------
# HB argument: an ImageBuf/ImageSpec handed to a pool task is published by the queue spin_mutex
# plus ImageBufImpl's own atomic refcount, so payload/refcount/spec scalars read by the worker
# are ordered — but both synchronisation sources live in the uninstrumented archive.
race:OpenImageIO::v3_1::ImageBufImpl
# HB argument: ImageBuf lifetime (impl_deleter) after the last pool task dropped its reference.
race:OpenImageIO::v3_1::ImageBuf
# HB argument: pixel copy inside a pool task over a buffer published as above; the racing access
# is usually the memcpy/memset interceptor, hence any-frame matching.
race:OpenImageIO::v3_1::copy_image
# HB argument: `interppixel<float>()` — OIIO's own interpolation helper inside
# ImageBufAlgo::resample, run by a pool worker on a buffer published through the queue spin_mutex;
# the paired free is the OIIO pixel buffer (std::unique_ptr<char[]>, OIIO-only storage) released
# by the calling thread after the synchronous resample returned. Cross-checked with the ASan+UBSan
# dev build (build/m2-t1/pp_test_thumbs ×3, ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1): zero
# heap-use-after-free reports, i.e. the buffer is genuinely not used after it is freed.
race:interppixel
# HB argument: OIIO string_view constructed over a pooled spec's std::string.
race:OpenImageIO::v3_1::basic_string_view
# HB argument: TypeDesc/ImageSpec scalar readers of a pooled spec (elementsize/size/channel_bytes).
race:OpenImageIO::v3_1::TypeDesc
